  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe pattern across the CONUS this afternoon consists of a large mid-\nlevel ridge centered over the southeastern US, southwest flow aloft \nfrom the Desert Southwest into the High Plains and Midwest, a compact\nlow over south-central Canada and a trough axis off the Pacific \nNorthwest coastline. There is also a tropical system just south of \nLouisiana that is set to impact portions of eastern TX early this \nweek. At the sfc, there remains an inverted trough/weak cold front across\nfar northern KS where temperatures are a few degrees cooler. Most \nlocations south of that boundary are approaching 100 degrees this\nafternoon with heat index values a few degrees higher. The current \nHeat Advisory continues until 7PM today.\n\nThe sfc boundary looks to push northward tonight as a LLJ develops, \nkeeping any rain chances north of the area. A very similar pattern \nlooks to remain in place through the rest of the workweek, allowing \nfor south/southwesterly flow and very warm 925-850mb temps to keep \nwell above average temps in place heading into early September. \nAfternoon highs should be around 100 degrees each day with overnight \nlows in the mid to upper 70s. For comparison, average highs are \ncurrently in the mid 80s and lows in the mid 60s. Dew points each \nafternoon should range from the mid to upper 60s, which will cause \nheat index values to stay in the 100 to 105 degree range. Have opted \nto not continue the Heat Advisory with marginal conditions in place \nmost afternoons this week. That said, however, those spending time \noutdoors should remain cautious of the heat and take proper steps to \navoid heat related illnesses that can set in quickly.\n\nLong range models seem to be in agreement with this hot and dry \nstretch of weather continuing through the weekend and likely into \nnext week as the upper ridge expands directly over the central US. \nThe NBM spread in high temperatures increases slightly toward early \nnext week and the going forecast tries to lower temps as a potential \nsfc cold front approaches the area. Am thinking that front might get \nwashed out before it can make a difference and that we will see the\ntemps trend back toward triple digits as that timeframe nears.",
